  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1235 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nThe latest water vapor loop was showing a jet diving south through \nthe Western High Plains early this afternoon with troughing \ndeveloping across the western Dakotas and western NE. Low to mid \nlevel warm and moist advection will increase through the afternoon, \nand remain locked in place over far eastern SD into parts of \nsouthwest MN and northwest IA into the evening. Right now it appears \nthat this mid level front (roughly 850-750 mb) will not move much \nthrough the evening and early overnight hours as the main vorticity \nrotates slowly southward, pivoting roughly over far eastern SD, \nbefore dropping south of the area early Wednesday morning. With this \nin mind and combing through the latest model and AI data, have upped \nsnowfall amounts a touch and shifted them westward towards I-29. \nThis is in response to upper wave pivoting nearby and the locked in \nlow to mid level boundary. Overall, the location within this band \nappears to saturate and snow for about 6 to 12 hours, so even at a \ntenth of an inch of an hour that would produce a half an inch to an \ninch. While there is not really any instability above the front to \nspeak of, some stronger forcing within this warm/moist advection \ncould produce snowfall rates of quarter to a half an inch per hour \nwhich would aid in bumping up snowfall amounts closer to 2 inches. \nAnd finally, if this front does completely lock into place for 3-6 \nhours, a few locations could creep up to around 3 inches. \n\nFrom roughly 6-12z saturation in the dgz will diminish from \nnorthwest to southeast, bringing an end to snowfall. All told, most \nlocations east of the James River will see a half an inch to an \ninch, with a small area of 2\" or so closer to I-29.\n\nDrier air in the low and mid levels settles in on Wednesday and with \nthe thought that we will see 1-2\" of snow cover in a few areas, \nlowered highs 2-4 degrees in those locations, mostly far eastern SD \ninto northwest IA.\n\nThursday will see low pressure deepen to the southeast of the area \nas a new wave moves into the Northern Rockies. This will leave the \nimmediate vicinity with some quiet and cool weather. Lowered morning \nlows a bit with lighter winds and some snow cover still possible and \nlowered highs a degree or two with what is likely to be left of the \nsnow cover.\n\nA westerly upper level jet will dive through the area Friday into \nSaturday. This will likely bring through a brief shot of lift on \nFriday, but for now precipitation is not expected. The dgz has \nclimbed to 12-16 kft AGL, so while that does appear to saturate for \na few hours, the air below is pretty dry. Temperatures climb back to \naround normal both days with highs from the upper 30s to mid 40s.\n\nSunday night into Monday another wave will move through NE and will \nswing a brief shot of warm air advection into the area Sunday night \ninto Monday morning. For now the air mass looks a little too dry so \nwill not have any precipitation chances in the forecast.",
